outline.description:
In November 2020 Justin Cutbank awakened his girlfriend D.F., held her at gunpoint during an intoxication episode, forced her toward her housemates’ children, fled taking her cell phone and weapon(s), was later arrested when officers entered a second residence where he barricaded himself.
Subsequently police recovered his stolen cell phone location trail‑data/pathway trace revealing a Marlin ® 22‑caliber rifle fitted with a sawed‑off barrel–stock whose DNA matched Cutbank.
At trial D.F.; C.F.; others identified this rifle as one Cutbank had carried moments prior;
Jury convicted him under FFPEC statutes §§922(g)(I)&924(e); certified him responsible for three violent felony convictions qualifying him for ACCA enhancement;
District court sentenced him sixty‑seven months—aligned with advisory guideline range fifteen‑year schedule—and he appealed arguing procedural/evidentiary abuse.

Defense moved pre‑trial disallowing D.F.’s observation that Cutbank had previously possessed the sawed‑off rifle—a straight factual admission establishing ownership/control pertinent “prior possession” doctrine affirmed via United States v.Battle interprets intrinsic firearms evidence permitted;
District court upheld admission knowing it falls into ‘ownership control’ exception exempt from propensity limitation;
No abuse exercised.

Defendant argued inadmissible admissions made publicly voiced toward police shouldn’t influence jury absent maker testify;
Court pivoted from preemptive limiting instruction argument citing Bennett precedent:“Judge need not bind inculpate hearing until declarants testify;” complemented Charboneau doctrinal affirmation;
Assured Guest presented inferior homicide confession dentistry concluded
understood constitutional scheme satisfied…no prejudice.
